 							<h1 class="major">Privacy &amp; AI Governance Self-Assessment</h1>
 							<section>
-								<p>Six practitioner-built assessments, modeled on the kind of tools used in real privacy, AI governance and third-party risk programs. The <strong>Privacy Assessment</strong> screens a process end to end - what data, whose, for what purposes, on what lawful basis, with what retention, who it is shared with and where it goes - and tells you whether a DPIA is needed; if it is, it hands over to the <strong>Full DPIA</strong> - necessity and proportionality, individual rights, transfers, the harms the processing could cause individuals rated before and after mitigation, and the three feared security events, all plotted on one severity &times; likelihood matrix. Where a process relies on legitimate interests, the <strong>Legitimate Interest Test</strong> works through the purpose, necessity and balancing tests and shows both sides of the balance. The <strong>AI Risk Assessment</strong> classifies an AI system against the EU AI Act, and the <strong>Incident &amp; Breach Severity Assessment</strong> records an incident and calculates its severity score using the published ENISA method, with an analysis of what drives the score and what would change it. The <strong>Third-Party Security Assessment</strong> is a detailed vendor security questionnaire - governance, people, assets, development, access, operations, cryptography, incident response, physical security, continuity, compliance, and an AI/ML supplement - scored as inherent risk tempered by control maturity, with a full list of gaps to close. Everything runs in your browser - nothing is submitted anywhere, and your saved assessments stay on your own device, exportable as JSON or CSV. Looking for the underlying guidance rather than a scored assessment? Jump to <a href="practical-privacy.html">Practical Privacy</a> for an everyday playbook of genericized, practitioner-tested guidance, or <a href="practical-ai-act-advice.html">Practical AI Act Advice</a> for a working reference on EU AI Act scope, obligations and enforcement.</p>
+								<p>Nine practitioner-built assessments, modeled on the kind of tools used in real privacy, AI governance and third-party risk programs. The <strong>Privacy Assessment</strong> screens a process end to end - what data, whose, for what purposes, on what lawful basis, with what retention, who it is shared with and where it goes - and tells you whether a DPIA is needed; if it is, it hands over to the <strong>Full DPIA</strong> - necessity and proportionality, individual rights, transfers, the harms the processing could cause individuals rated before and after mitigation, and the three feared security events, all plotted on one severity &times; likelihood matrix. Where a process relies on legitimate interests, the <strong>Legitimate Interest Test</strong> works through the purpose, necessity and balancing tests and shows both sides of the balance. The <strong>AI Risk Assessment</strong> classifies an AI system against the EU AI Act, and the <strong>Incident &amp; Breach Severity Assessment</strong> records an incident and calculates its severity score using the published ENISA method, with an analysis of what drives the score and what would change it. The <strong>Third-Party Security Assessment</strong> is a detailed vendor security questionnaire - governance, people, assets, development, access, operations, cryptography, incident response, physical security, continuity, compliance, and an AI/ML supplement - scored as inherent risk tempered by control maturity, with a full list of gaps to close. Three more cover newer obligations: the <strong>Fundamental Rights Impact Assessment</strong> for deployers of high-risk AI under Article 27 of the EU AI Act, which can start from a DPIA saved on your device; the <strong>Generative AI Risk Assessment</strong>, which maps its gaps to the NIST Generative AI Profile and the OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications; and the <strong>Transfer Impact Assessment</strong>, which follows the six steps of EDPB Recommendations 01/2020. Everything runs in your browser - nothing is submitted anywhere, and your saved assessments stay on your own device, exportable as JSON or CSV. Looking for the underlying guidance rather than a scored assessment? Jump to <a href="practical-privacy.html">Practical Privacy</a> for an everyday playbook of genericized, practitioner-tested guidance, or <a href="practical-ai-act-advice.html">Practical AI Act Advice</a> for a working reference on EU AI Act scope, obligations and enforcement.</p>
